# What is Archnaut?

Archnaut is a repo-installed architecture management layer for AI-assisted development.
It is an npm package (`archnaut`) consisting of:
- A CLI (`archnaut init/start/stop/status/scan`) built with Node.js + Commander.js
- A background daemon that self-daemonizes (detached child, PID in `.archnaut/daemon.pid`)
- An MCP server (Streamable HTTP on `localhost:7070/api/mcp`) using `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk`
- A stdio shim (`mcp-shim`) that proxies JSON-RPC to the HTTP endpoint for tools requiring stdio
- A local web UI (React + Vite SPA, pre-built, bundled inside the npm package, served via `sirv`)
- A React Flow (`@xyflow/react`) diagram engine with ELK.js auto-layout in a Web Worker
- A `better-sqlite3` embedded SQLite runtime store (`.archnaut/db.sqlite`, never committed)
- A rules/hooks/skills engine that installs config files into the user's repo at `archnaut init` time

All served on a single port (7070) with path-based routing:
- `localhost:7070/`        → sirv(dist/) [static SPA]
- `localhost:7070/api/*`   → MCP Streamable HTTP transport
- `localhost:7070/ws`      → WebSocket (live diagram push)

# Source of Truth

`PROJECT.md` at the repo root is the authoritative product definition. It covers the full intended product, v0.1 vs later scope, feature specs, and design decisions. Agents and contributors should read and follow `PROJECT.md` when deciding what to build, what is in scope, and how features should behave.

`CONTRIBUTING.md` is the project style guide — coding conventions, commit message format, PR workflow, and development setup. Follow it when writing code, naming commits, and structuring changes.

This rule file is a condensed agent reference — on any conflict, `PROJECT.md` wins for scope and architecture; `CONTRIBUTING.md` wins for style and workflow.

# Repository Structure

This is a Node.js monorepo. Package manager: pnpm workspaces.

```
/
├── packages/
│   ├── cli/           # CLI entry point — Commander.js, archnaut init/start/stop/status/scan
│   ├── daemon/        # Self-daemonizing background process manager
│   ├── mcp-server/    # MCP server, read-tools, write-tools
│   ├── mcp-shim/      # Thin stdio-to-HTTP proxy shim
│   ├── web-ui/        # React + Vite SPA (pre-built dist/ bundled into npm package)
│   ├── rules-engine/  # Generates and installs AI tool config files
│   └── shared/        # Shared TypeScript types and schemas
├── PROJECT.md          # Authoritative product definition (source of truth for scope & specs)
├── CONTRIBUTING.md     # Project style guide (coding conventions, commits, PR workflow)
├── archnaut.json       # Canonical committed architecture artifact (do not gitignore)
└── .archnaut/          # Runtime state — ALL gitignored (db.sqlite, daemon.pid, daemon.log)
```

# Module Boundaries — Never Cross These

- `cli` may import from `daemon`, `rules-engine`, `shared` — nothing else
- `mcp-server` may import from `shared` — NOT from `cli` or `web-ui`
- `web-ui` may import from `shared` — NOT from `cli` or `mcp-server`
- `daemon` has no imports from other packages — it manages process lifecycle only
- `mcp-shim` has no imports from other packages — it is a stateless proxy

# Locked Technology Decisions

Do NOT suggest alternatives to these. They are decided and documented.

| Concern | Decision | Rejected alternatives |
|---|---|---|
| Diagram engine | `@xyflow/react` (React Flow) | Cytoscape.js, D3.js |
| Auto-layout | ELK.js `layered` in a Web Worker | dagre, cola |
| Static serving | `sirv` SPA mode | express.static, http-server |
| Runtime DB | `better-sqlite3` (in-process, no port) | PostgreSQL, Prisma, networked DB |
| CLI prompts | `@clack/prompts` | inquirer, prompts |
| MCP SDK | `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` (official) | custom MCP implementation |
| Default port | `7070` | 3000, 4200 (Angular conflict) |
| Config format | JSON (`archnaut.json`) | YAML, TOML |
| IPC sync | Chokidar file watcher → WebSocket `/ws` | polling, SSE |
| Process model | Single background process, single port | IDE extension, per-command spawn |

# Architecture Rules

1. The MCP server is the **only mutation authority** for `archnaut.json` and the SQLite DB.
2. Agents NEVER write directly to `archnaut.json` or `.archnaut/db.sqlite`.
3. All state changes go through MCP tools owned by the server.
4. `archnaut.json` is the canonical git-tracked artifact. The SQLite DB is a runtime projection — never committed, always rebuildable from JSON.
5. Deterministic normalization (sort by id: workspaces → nodes → edges → concerns) runs before every write to `archnaut.json`.
6. The daemon does NOT auto-start via git hooks or agent calls. Developer runs `archnaut start` explicitly.
7. Pre-commit hook is a **soft warn only** — it never blocks commits.
8. Agents do NOT spawn the daemon — they connect to an already-running daemon at `localhost:7070`.

# ID Conventions

- Workspace: `ws.<name>` → `ws.api`, `ws.web`
- Component node: `cmp.<workspace>.<name>` → `cmp.api.checkout`
- Database: `db.<name>` → `db.users`
- External service: `ext.<name>` → `ext.stripe`
- Queue: `queue.<name>` → `queue.events`
- Edge: `edge.<from>-<to>` → `edge.checkout-stripe`

IDs are assigned and preserved by the MCP server. Never generate IDs outside the server.

# MCP Tools Reference (v0.1)

**Read tools:**
- `getarchitecture` — full architecture graph as structured context
- `getcomponentcontext(name)` — focused context for a single component
- `getplannedfeatures` — all components marked planned but not yet implemented

**Write tools (agents call these — never direct file/DB writes):**
- `begin_task(taskId, agentId, summary, targetNodeIds, [plannedFeatureIds])` — declare task start
- `addnode(component)` — add a discovered or planned component
- `addedge(from, to, type)` — add a dependency relationship
- `setnodemetadata(id, metadata)` — enrich a node with details
- `markimplemented(featureId)` — low-level state transition (UI-facing; agents prefer complete_task)
- `complete_task(taskId, status, [implementedNodeIds], [notes])` — finalize task, trigger normalization
- `updatearchitecture` — trigger full rescan prompt
- `flagconcern(description)` — surface architectural concern for human review
- `cleararchitecture` — reset diagram before full rescan

# Agent Workflow Pattern

When implementing a feature:
1. Call `getarchitecture` to read current state (requires daemon running)
2. Call `begin_task` with `taskId`, `agentId`, `summary`, `targetNodeIds`
3. Implement the feature; call `addnode`/`addedge`/`setnodemetadata` as needed
4. Call `complete_task` with `status: "completed"` and `implementedNodeIds`
5. Never skip `complete_task` — it triggers normalization and persistence

# Session handoff (`CURRENT.md`)

- `CURRENT.md` is a session handoff summary, not a live log.
- Update or regenerate it **at the end of a coherent task or session** (feature implemented, refactor completed).
- Do NOT rewrite it on every small code change; git history captures fine‑grained edits.

# v0.1 Scope — What Ships Now vs Later

See `PROJECT.md` (MVP Scope section) for the full authoritative v0.1 definition. Summary below:

**In v0.1 (implement these):**
- `archnaut init/start/stop/status/scan` CLI commands
- Self-daemonizing background process
- MCP server with all read + write tools listed above
- stdio shim for tools requiring subprocess transport
- Live block diagram (React Flow + ELK.js)
- Component inspector (click node → files, dependencies, notes)
- Drag-and-drop planned node creation (design mode)
- Rules/hooks/skills engine + `archnaut init` tool detection + multi-select prompt
- Pre-commit hooks (daemon health check + stale diagram warning — both soft warns)
- Session handoff (`CURRENT.md` generation at end of agent session)
- Scan skill installed at `.archnaut/skills/scan.md`

**Post-v0.1 — do NOT implement these now:**
- `archnaut upgrade` (v0.2)
- Diff view (planned vs implemented overlay)
- Drift alerts (visual indicators)
- Token-aware context injection
- Anti-pattern detection
- Real-time multi-developer collaboration (CRDT/OT)
- `hookPolicy: block-on-daemon-down` enforcement
- `onlyRenderVisibleElements` for 500+ node monorepos

